window.SLIDE_TEXT["cad-part-3"] = {"1":"<ul><li>Coronary Artery Disease Part III:</li><li>Clinical Presentation, Diagnostic Evaluation, and Risk Stratification of the Chest Pain Patient</li><li>PHAS 517</li><li>Jessica Stager, MPAS, PA-C</li><li>November 18, 2026</li></ul>",
"2":"<ul><li>Chest Pain</li><li>Chest pain is the 2nd most common reason for adults to present to the ED in the United States</li><li>Chest pain also leads to nearly 4 million outpatient visits annually in the United States</li><li>Although the cause of chest pain is often noncardiac, CAD affects &gt;18.2 million adults in the United States and remains the leading cause of death for men and women, accounting for &gt;365 000 deaths annually</li><li>Distinguishing between serious and benign causes of chest pain is imperative</li></ul>",
"3":"<ul><li>Angina Pectoris</li><li>Angina pectoris is chest pain or discomfort caused by myocardial ischemia</li><li>Patients describe retrosternal pressure, tightness, or heaviness—not always \"pain\"—with radiation to arm, jaw, shoulder, or back</li><li>Associated symptoms include dyspnea, diaphoresis, and nausea</li><li>\"Silent\" ischemia can occur in elderly and diabetic patients</li></ul>",
"4":"<ul><li>Angina Pectoris</li><li>Stable angina: Triggered by exertion or emotional stress; resolves with rest; caused by fixed atherosclerotic obstruction with subendocardial ischemia</li><li>Unstable angina: Occurs at rest or with minimal exertion; indicates plaque rupture with thrombosis; medical emergency</li><li>Vasospastic (Prinzmetal) angina: Caused by coronary artery spasm; can occur at rest; causes transmural ischemia with ST-segment elevation</li></ul>",
"5":"",
"6":"<ul><li>Symptoms and Signs of Cardiac Ischemia</li><li>Other symptoms on history that may suggest coronary artery disease:<ul><li>Progressively worsening dyspnea or exercise intolerance</li><li>Palpitations, lightheadedness</li><li>Weight gain (days) or lower extremity edema</li></ul></li><li>Symptoms do not reliably correspond with the level of disease.  Sudden cardiac death is sometimes the first symptom of CAD.</li></ul>",
"7":"<ul><li>Anginal Equivalents</li><li>An anginal equivalent is a symptom other than chest discomfort that may reflect myocardial ischemia<ul><li>Common anginal equivalents:<ul><li>Dyspnea<ul><li>Unusual fatigue or reduced exercise tolerance<ul><li>Exertional weakness or lightheadedness<ul><li>Nausea or upper abdominal discomfort</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul></li></ul></li></ul></li></ul>",
"8":"<ul><li>CAD in Women</li><li>Women are more likely to report non-specific associated symptoms – nausea/vomiting, abdominal pain, shortness of breath, severe fatigue</li><li>During an MI, females are more likely than males to present without chest pain</li></ul>",
"9":"",
"10":"<ul><li>CAD in Older Patients</li><li>Although patients &gt;75 years of age account for 33% of all cases of ACS, alternative diagnoses are still more common than a cardiac cause of chest pain at presentation</li><li>Patients &gt;75 years of age may have symptoms of shortness of breath, syncope, mental impairment, or abdominal pain, or experienced an unexplained fall</li></ul>",
"11":"<ul><li>Chest Pain Classification</li><li>2021 AHA/ACC Chest Pain Guideline:</li><li>Cardiac - Chest pain occurring because of an underlying cardiac etiology. Includes classic chest discomfort based on quality, location, radiation, and provoking and relieving factors that makes it more likely to be of cardiac ischemic origin.</li><li>Possible Cardiac - Chest pain symptoms that suggest a cardiac origin</li><li>Noncardiac - Chest pain symptoms likely because of a noncardiac cause in patients with persistent or recurring symptoms despite a negative stress test or anatomic cardiac evaluation, or a low-risk designation by a clinical decision pathway</li></ul>",
"12":"<ul><li>Chest Pain Classification</li><li>Diamond-Forrester Criteria: (no longer recommended)</li><li>Typical Angina:<ul><li>SUBSTERNAL CHEST PAIN</li><li>EXERTIONAL CHEST PAIN</li><li>CHEST PAIN RELIEVED WITH REST OR NITROGLYCERIN<ul><li>Anginal Chest Pain<ul><li>All 3 OF THE ABOVE</li></ul></li><li>Atypical Anginal Chest Pain<ul><li>2 OF THE ABOVE</li></ul></li><li>Non-Anginal Chest pain<ul><li>1 OF THE ABOVE</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul></li></ul>",
"13":"<ul><li>Signs of Cardiac Ischemia</li><li>The physical exam is normal if the person is not having active cardiac ischemia.</li><li>When ischemia is present:<ul><li>The patient often appears uncomfortable, pale, diaphoretic</li><li>Dyspnea, increased work of breathing, conversational dyspnea, rales</li><li>Altered mentation</li><li>Additional heart sounds- gallops<ul><li>S4 due to decreased LV compliance (TN)</li><li>Less often, S3 due to LV systolic dysfxn (KY)</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul>",
"14":"<ul><li>Differential Dx of Chest Pain</li><li>Pulmonary: pulmonary embolus, pneumothorax, pneumonia, pleurisy</li><li>GI: GERD/PUD, esophagitis, pancreatitis, esophageal spasm, biliary colic, cholangitis</li><li>Chest wall/ MSK: costochondritis, rib fracture</li><li>Non-ischemic CV: pericarditis, aortic dissection</li><li>Psychiatric: anxiety, hyperventilation, panic disorder, conversion disorder</li></ul>",
"15":"",
"16":"",
"17":"<ul><li>Symptoms Suggesting an Alternate Dx</li><li>Pleuritic pain, sharp or knife-like pain related to respiratory movements or cough think pulmonary cause or musculoskeletal chest pain.</li><li>Primary or sole location in the mid or lower abdominal region  think GI issues, dyspepsia, GERD, PUD, esophagitis, pancreatitis.</li><li>Any discomfort localized with one finger  think musculoskeletal chest pain.</li></ul>",
"18":"<ul><li>Symptoms Suggesting an Alternate Dx</li><li>Any discomfort reproduced by movement or palpation think musculoskeletal chest pain</li><li>Constant pain lasting for days pulmonary or musculoskeletal CP.</li><li>Fleeting pains lasting for 30 seconds or less  nerve pain (musculoskeletal).</li><li>Pain radiating into the lower extremities or above the mandible  musculoskeletal pain.</li></ul>",
"19":"",
"20":"<ul><li>Symptoms Suggesting an Alternate Dx</li><li>A 58-year old man presents with substernal pressure that occurs when walking uphill or carrying groceries. The discomfort sometimes radiates to his left shoulder and resolves within 5 minutes of rest. He has a history of HTN, hyperlipidemia and a 25 pack year smoking history.</li><li>Is this presentation more suggestive of cardiac ischemia or a noncardiac etiology?</li><li>Which features are most concerning?</li></ul>",
"21":"<ul><li>So, you have a patient c/o chest pain in your outpatient office and myocardial ischemia is on the differential..</li><li>Patient comes to your office with chest pain</li><li>Patient had chest pain previously, but is not currently in pain</li><li>Unstable angina features- call cardiology and get them in ASAP or send patient to ER</li><li>Risk stratify with stress testing</li><li>Patient is CURRENTLY having chest pain</li><li>Call 911.  Send patient to the ER by ambulance</li></ul>",
"22":"<ul><li>What Are the Clinical Syndromes of Symptomatic CAD?</li><li>There are four disease manifestations which may occur due to the process of coronary atherosclerosis - numbers 2, 3 and 4 are considered acute coronary syndromes (ACS).<ul><li>Stable angina<ul><li>Unstable angina (UA)</li><li>Non-ST elevation MI or NSTEMI</li><li>ST-segment elevation MI or STEMI</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul>",
"23":"<ul><li>Symptomatic CAD: Stable Angina</li><li>Stable angina: is characterized by predictable and reproducible symptoms of cardiac ischemia induced by exercise/exertion, cold exposure, or emotional stress.</li><li>Symptoms typically last 5-10 minutes and are relieved by rest or nitroglycerin.</li><li>Stable angina most commonly results from a fixed, flow-limiting atherosclerotic plaque that limits coronary blood flow during periods of increased myocardial oxygen demand.</li></ul>",
"24":"<ul><li>Symptomatic CAD: Stable Angina</li></ul>",
"25":"<ul><li>Stable Angina Example</li><li>Thomas is a 72-year-old man with history of HTN, HLD, DM and tobacco abuse.  He has had a previous heart attack 6 years ago and at that time had a 3-vessel CABG.  He goes to his cardiologist for follow-up, and confirms that he is taking his ASA, metoprolol and losartan as prescribed.  He also reports that he has substernal CP when he walks more than 15 minutes on his treadmill, but this is about the same as it was 6 months ago, and he continues to walk 13 minutes, then rest for 5, before beginning to walk again.  His exam is normal today and his script for SL nitro is renewed before he leaves the office.</li></ul>",
"26":"<ul><li>Acute Coronary Syndromes</li><li>The acute coronary syndromes include:<ul><li>Unstable angina<ul><li>Non-ST elevation MI or NSTEMI</li><li>ST elevation MI or STEMI</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>ACS may be caused by:<ul><li>Sudden atherosclerotic plaque rupture.</li><li>Growth of the plaque to a critical level.</li><li>Stress from a non-cardiac illness that taxes the diseased heart’s ability to cope.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"27":"<ul><li>Acute Coronary Syndromes</li><li>Acute coronary syndromes are marked by an increase in the symptoms of cardiac ischemia in terms of:<ul><li>Frequency</li><li>Severity</li><li>Duration</li><li>Provocation - occurring with measurably less exertion or possibly occurring at rest.</li><li>Relief - the symptoms are less responsive to rest or SL nitroglycerin.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"28":"<ul><li>Acute Coronary Syndromes</li><li>Unstable angina: The patient is having ischemic symptoms suggestive of an ACS but has<ul><li>No elevation in troponin<ul><li>No definitive EKG changes indicative of ischemia on the 12 lead EKG, ST segment depression or new T wave inversion may be seen, no ST elevation</li><li>These patients are considered to have unstable angina, but all ACS patients begin this way, so this diagnosis evolves over time.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul>",
"29":"<ul><li>Unstable Angina Example</li><li>Thomas returns to the office 4 months later and reports that he is worse.  About 2 weeks ago he had his usual CP but was only walking into the store from the parking lot.  He sat down inside the store, and the pain went away.  4 days ago, he had another episode of chest pain, but this time was sitting on the couch, watching college football.  It finally resolved after 2 nitro tablets taken 5 minutes apart, but his wife made him come into the office today.  Vitals and exam are normal and EKG in the office shows sinus rhythm without ST or T wave changes.</li></ul>",
"30":"<ul><li>NSTEMI: the patient is having ischemic symptoms suggestive of an ACS and<ul><li>an elevation in troponin</li><li>with or without ECG changes indicative of ischemia, but no ST segment elevation have an NSTEMI.</li></ul></li><li>NSTEMI and UA may look alike at first until the HS troponin levels begin to rise.</li><li>Acute Coronary Syndromes</li></ul>",
"31":"<ul><li>NSTEMI</li></ul>",
"32":"<ul><li>STEMI: the hat trick . . . patients have<ul><li>Symptoms of cardiac ischemia, and</li><li>Elevated troponin and</li><li>ST segment elevation on the 12 lead EKG</li></ul></li><li>Acute Coronary Syndromes</li></ul>",
"33":"<ul><li>NSTEMI Example</li><li>Because Thomas is not actively having chest pain, he is scheduled for an outpatient left heart cath in 5 days. However, he goes to the ER by ambulance 2 days after his office visit at 11pm with typical chest pain that is more severe and doesn’t resolve with SL nitro x 3.</li><li>NSTEMI: In the ER, Thomas is found to have a HS troponin of 527 (normal &lt; 15), and T wave inversions in the lateral leads.  A 90-min repeat troponin is 1,637.  A heparin gtt is started, and a LHC is scheduled for 9 am.</li></ul>",
"34":"<ul><li>STEMI Example</li><li>Because Thomas is not actively having chest pain, he is scheduled for an outpatient left heart cath in 5 days. However, he goes to the ER by ambulance 2 days after his office visit at 11pm with typical chest pain that is more severe and doesn’t resolve with nitro x 3.</li><li>STEMI: In the ER, Thomas is found to have a HS troponin of 527 (normal &lt; 15), and ST segment elevation in the anterolateral leads.  He is taken to the cath lab within 90 minutes of ER arrival for emergent LHC and stent placement at 12:15am.</li></ul>",
"35":"<ul><li>ACS Summary</li></ul><table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>Condition</td><td>Coronary blood flow</td><td>Myocardial injury</td></tr><tr><td>Unstable angina</td><td>Reduced, often transient</td><td>No acute myocardial injury</td></tr><tr><td>NSTEMI</td><td>Reduced; partial occlusion may occur</td><td>Acute myocardial injury</td></tr><tr><td>STEMI</td><td>Often acute coronary occlusion</td><td>Acute myocardial injury with a qualifying ST-elevation pattern</td></tr></table>",
"36":"<ul><li>Initial Evaluation of the Chest Pain Patient</li><li>Clinical history of cardiac chest pain:<ul><li>O:</li><li>L:</li><li>D:</li><li>C:</li><li>A:</li><li>R:</li><li>T:</li><li>S:</li></ul></li></ul>",
"37":"<ul><li>Initial Evaluation of the Chest Pain Patient</li><li>Use OLDCARTS to organize the history of present illness</li><li>O</li><li>Onset</li><li>When did it start? Sudden or gradual? What were you doing?</li><li>L</li><li>Location</li><li>Where is the discomfort? Can you point to it?</li><li>D</li><li>Duration</li><li>How long does each episode last? Constant or intermittent?</li><li>C</li><li>Character</li><li>Pressure, tightness, squeezing, heaviness, burning, or sharp?</li><li>A</li><li>Aggravating / relieving factors</li><li>Exertion, stress, meals, position, breathing? Does rest help?</li><li>R</li><li>Radiation</li><li>Does it spread to the arm, shoulder, jaw, neck, back, or epigastrium?</li><li>T</li><li>Timing / pattern</li><li>How often? Any change in frequency, duration, or severity?</li><li>S</li><li>Severity</li><li>Rate 0–10. How does it limit usual activity?</li><li>Do not stop at OLDCARTS</li><li>Ask about associated symptoms (dyspnea, diaphoresis, nausea, palpitations, syncope), prior episodes, CAD risk factors, and cardiac history.</li></ul>",
"38":"<ul><li>Initial Evaluation of the Chest Pain Patient</li><li>Do they have cardiovascular risk factors that make CAD more likely?</li></ul><table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>Non-Modifiable</td><td>Modifiable</td></tr><tr><td>Age</td><td>Hyperlipidemia</td></tr><tr><td>Male Sex</td><td>Hypertension</td></tr><tr><td>Family history of premature heart disease Males &lt; 55 y/o Females &lt; 65 y/o</td><td>Diabetes mellitus</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Metabolic Syndrome</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Cigarette Smoking</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Obesity/Sedentary Lifestyle</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Excessive alcohol intake</td></tr></table>",
"39":"<ul><li>Initial Evaluation of the Chest Pain Patient</li><li>Physical Exam: Does the patient have any evidence of cardiovascular disease?<ul><li>Carotid bruit<ul><li>Poor peripheral pulses</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Initial Testing:<ul><li>Resting 12 lead EKG at presentation</li><li>Serial cardiac troponin</li><li>Usual CBC, CMP</li><li>Other testing to eval differentials - such as CXR.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"40":"<ul><li>Resting 12 lead EKG</li><li>Resting EKG abnormalities which are concerning for possible coronary ischemia:<ul><li>T wave inversion- non-specific</li><li>ST-depression- maybe . . .</li><li>ST elevation- definitive!</li><li>New left bundle branch block</li><li>Left ventricular hypertrophy and PVCs</li></ul></li></ul>",
"41":"<ul><li>Resting 12 lead EKG</li><li>A normal initial 12-lead EKG does not rule out ACS.</li><li>Obtain serial ECGs when symptoms persist or recur, or when clinical suspicion remains high</li></ul>",
"42":"<ul><li>EKG with T-wave INVERSIONS</li></ul>",
"43":"<ul><li>EKG with ST Depression</li></ul>",
"44":"<ul><li>Troponin I and T</li><li>Troponin is a protein found in muscle cells that contains three subunits: T, C, and I</li><li>Troponin is spilled into the circulation from damaged cardiac myocytes during myocardial ischemia.  It can also be “leaked” when there is significant cardiac stress.  For example- tachycardic dysrhythmias, RV failure from a PE, and decompensated HF</li></ul>",
"45":"<ul><li>Troponin I</li><li>Troponin I is found only in cardiac cells and is therefore the most specific to cardiac muscle damage.</li><li>Troponin I becomes detectable in the serum between 4 -6 hours after onset of cardiac ischemia.</li><li>Troponin peaks and then falls to lower levels and remains elevated at these low levels for 7-10 days after an event if checked in a regular sensitivity assay.</li></ul>",
"46":"<ul><li>Troponin I</li><li>Troponin is both sensitive and specific for MI in the right clinical circumstances<ul><li>Sensitivity: 50% at 4 hours, 97% at 6 hours</li><li>Specificity: 95%</li></ul></li><li>Troponin is chronically elevated in ESRD and CKD stage IV because of impaired clearance.  Look for a prior level to determine if the patient is above their usual baseline.</li><li>An initial troponin can be negative/ normal, if the CP patient presented to the ER quickly.</li></ul>",
"47":"<ul><li>High Sensitivity Troponin</li><li>In 2021, new guidelines were published by the ACC recommending the use of high sensitivity troponin for the evaluation of chest pain.</li><li>Both high-sensitivity and traditional troponin assays measure the exact same molecule, but HS assays measure much more precisely and at much lower concentrations.<ul><li>With traditional troponin assays, a troponin level was not detectable in most patients who lacked cardiac ischemia.  To qualify as a high-sensitivity troponin assay, the test must be able to detect a level of cardiac troponin in &gt;50% of normal individuals. This means that the majority of patients without cardiac ischemia will have a detectable level of cardiac troponin.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"48":"<ul><li>High Sensitivity Troponin</li><li>High-sensitivity troponin assays have significantly higher sensitivity for ACS than conventional troponin assays.  This means that they have a much higher negative predictive value.</li><li>In other words no elevation of troponin, no acute MI</li><li>This allows clinicians to more quickly and accurately identify those CP patients who do not have ACS and who are at very low risk of 30-day MACE (major adverse cardiac events).</li></ul>",
"49":"<ul><li>High Sensitivity Troponin</li><li>However, the cost of the increased sensitivity is a decreased specificity, yielding a lower positive predictive value.</li><li>This means that more people with a non-cardiac cause for their symptoms, who happen to have a detectable troponin, will be evaluated for ACS/ CAD.<ul><li>Remember that HS troponin is detectable in &gt; 50% of asymptomatic individuals who are at their baseline health.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"50":"<ul><li>High Sensitivity Troponin</li><li>Therefore, HS assays expedite the evaluation of suspected ACS patients and determine who is low-risk and therefore able to discharge to home.<ul><li>There is good data supporting the use of a single HS troponin value in a patient presenting &gt; 2 hours after symptom onset.  If the troponin is less than the sex-specific 99th percentile, this substantially lessens the concern for ACS.</li><li>We also evaluate the delta (change) between 2 serial HS troponin levels, taken 90-120 minutes apart.  If the patient has 2 levels below the sex-specific 99th percentile and minimal change  discharge to home.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"51":"<ul><li>Brain Natriuretic Peptide (BNP)</li><li>Brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) is a natriuretic hormone initially identified in the brain but released primarily from the cardiac ventricles.</li><li>The final cleavage of the prohormone produces biologically active BNP as well as biologically inert N-terminal pro-BNP (NT-proBNP).</li><li>Facilities usually choose to measure one.  Levels of BNP and NT-pro-BNP are not comparable.</li></ul>",
"52":"<ul><li>Brain Natriuretic Peptide (BNP)</li><li>The release of BNP is increased in decompensated HF, as cells secrete BNP in response to the high ventricular filling pressures to promote sodium wasting in the urine</li><li>BNP has a diuretic and natriuretic effect, inhibits the RAAS and sympathetic activity and has a positive inotropic effect on the ventricles.</li><li>BNP is renally cleared and thus NOT HELPFUL in AKI or significant CKD.  Always elevated.</li></ul>",
"53":"<ul><li>Initial Evaluation of the CP PatientPutting It Together</li><li>Step 1: Arrival and Triage</li><li>Assess airway, breathing, circulation, vital signs, oxygen saturation, and overall stability.</li><li>Identify ongoing severe pain, hypotension, respiratory distress, syncope, or other signs of instability.</li><li>Obtain a focused history: onset, location, character, duration, triggers, radiation, associated symptoms, and cardiovascular history.</li><li>Consider other life-threatening causes, including aortic dissection, pulmonary embolism, and tension pneumothorax.</li></ul>",
"54":"<ul><li>Initial Evaluation of the CP PatientPutting It Together</li><li>Step 2: Obtain a 12-lead EKG – within 10 minutes</li><li>Obtain and interpret the EKG within 10 minutes of arrival or first medical contact.</li><li>Look for ST-segment elevation or other findings concerning for acute ischemia.</li><li>If the initial EKG is nondiagnostic but suspicion remains, obtain serial EKGs.</li><li>Do not delay emergency reperfusion decisions while waiting for troponin results when the EKG and clinical presentation indicate an acute coronary occlusion.</li></ul>",
"55":"<ul><li>Initial Evaluation of the CP PatientPutting It Together</li><li>Step 3: Obtain initial labs and begin treatment</li><li>Obtain high-sensitivity cardiac troponin (hs-cTn).</li><li>Consider CBC, electrolytes/renal function, glucose, and other tests as indicated.</li><li>Give chewable ASA 325mg, when ACS is suspected and there is no contraindication.</li><li>Consider sublingual nitroglycerin for ongoing ischemic discomfort if hemodynamically appropriate and no contraindication exists.</li><li>Provide oxygen for hypoxemia; do not administer it routinely to patients with O2 &gt;90%.</li></ul>",
"56":"<ul><li>ER Evaluation of the Chest Pain Patient</li><li>If ST elevation is seen on the EKG in 2 or more anatomically contiguous leads and the patient has a clinical picture suggesting acute cardiac ischemia, then a STEMI is diagnosed.</li><li>Urgently notify interventional cardiology. Activate the STEMI/primary PCI pathway.</li><li>Administer aspirin and an appropriate P2Y12 inhibitor. (Ticagrelor or prasugrel is generally preferred over clopidogrel in patients undergoing PCI).</li><li>Proceed to the cath lab for coronary angiography and primary PCI when indicated.</li></ul>",
"57":"<ul><li>ER Evaluation of the Chest Pain Patient</li><li>If the initial EKG is normal or shows something other than ST elevation, an initial HS troponin will be obtained and repeated in 1-2 hours.</li><li>If the initial history and physical and the HS troponin is highly suggestive of ACS- the</li><li>cardiologist is called to the ER bedside to evaluate the patient.</li></ul>",
"58":"<ul><li>ER Evaluation of the Chest Pain Patient</li><li>Repeat EKGs should be obtained every 10-20 minutes or so in the ER patient who continues to have chest pain to look for STEMI.</li><li>These patients with an H&amp;P highly suggestive of ACS are typically admitted by the hospitalist service for UA/NSTEMI with cardiology consultation.</li></ul>",
"59":"<ul><li>ER Evaluation of the Chest Pain Patient</li><li>Management of ongoing ischemic chest pain:<ul><li>Sublingual nitroglycerin for symptom relief if hemodynamically stable.</li><li>IV nitroglycerin may be considered for persistent pain despite sublingual therapy, hypertension, or pulmonary edema.</li><li>Initiate oral beta blocker within 24 hours in patients with ACS if no contraindications.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"60":"<ul><li>ER Evaluation of the Chest Pain Patient</li><li>Anticoagulation for diagnosed ACS:<ul><li>Initiate parenteral anticoagulation unless contraindicated.</li><li>Options include unfractionated heparin (UFH) or enoxaparin (LMWH).</li><li>Choice depends on ACS type, treatment strategy, renal function, and clinical circumstances.</li></ul></li><li>Continue diagnostic evaluation<ul><li>Evaluate for other or concurrent causes of chest pain.</li><li>Reassess symptoms, EKG findings, hemodynamic status, and cardiac biomarkers.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"61":"<ul><li>ER Evaluation of the Chest Pain Patient</li><li>Many times, the patient who presents to the ER with chest pain resolves quickly and they become chest pain free.</li><li>It is unlikely that they are having ongoing ischemia . . . But we need to evaluate them still to determine if they are safe to send home with outpatient follow-up, or if they are high risk enough to admit to the hospital for a 23-hour observation stay for chest pain rule out.</li></ul>",
"62":"",
"63":"<ul><li>ER Evaluation of the Chest Pain Patient</li><li>The ER clinician now has to make a decision - is the patient high or low risk?</li><li>Calculate the HEART score.</li></ul><table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>History</td><td>Highly suspicious for ACS: 2 Moderately suspicious for ACS: 1 Not suspicious for ACS: 0</td><td>Risk Factors</td><td>3+ risk factors or a patient with known CAD: 2 1-2 risk factors: 1 No known risk factors: 0</td></tr><tr><td>EKG</td><td>Significant ST depression: 2 Non-specific repolarization disturbances/new LBBB/LVH: 1 Normal: 0</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Age</td><td>65+: 2 45-64: 1 Younger than 45: 0</td><td>Troponin</td><td>&gt;3x normal: 2 1-3x normal: 1 Normal: 0</td></tr></table>",
"64":"<ul><li>ER Evaluation of the Chest Pain Patient</li><li>0–3 points: Low risk</li><li>If EKG and appropriate troponin evaluation are reassuring, discharge may be appropriate. Routine urgent cardiac testing is generally unnecessary.</li><li>4–6 points: Intermediate risk</li><li>Continue evaluation with serial high-sensitivity troponins as indicated. ED observation or inpatient observation may be appropriate. Consider CCTA or stress testing based on clinical circumstances.</li><li>7–10 points: High risk</li><li>Requires further urgent evaluation and usually hospital-level care, depending on the clinical presentation.</li></ul>",
"65":"<ul><li>IP Evaluation of the Chest Pain Patient</li><li>The patient has been admitted for continued evaluation of chest pain / possible ACS.</li><li>The inpatient team will:<ul><li>Review the ED evaluation and obtain additional history as needed.</li><li>Reassess symptoms and perform a focused physical examination.</li><li>Review EKG findings and prior cardiac testing.</li><li>Continue cardiac monitoring when clinically indicated.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"66":"<ul><li>IP Evaluation of the Chest Pain Patient</li><li>High-sensitivity troponin (preferred):<ul><li>Initial measurement at presentation.</li><li>Repeat in 1–2 hours.</li><li>If intermediate risk, repeat again at 3–6 hours.</li></ul></li><li>Conventional troponin:<ul><li>Initial measurement at presentation.</li><li>Repeat in 3–6 hours.</li></ul></li><li>Evaluate the pattern of change (rise or fall) in troponin levels in conjunction with the patient's clinical presentation and EKG findings.</li></ul>",
"67":"<ul><li>After reviewing the patient's history, EKG, serial hs-troponins, and clinical risk, the hospitalist determines whether additional cardiac testing is necessary.</li><li>Scenario: The patient has no diagnostic ischemic EKG changes and serial hs-troponins show no significant elevation or rise/fall pattern.</li><li>IP Evaluation of the Chest Pain Patient</li></ul>",
"68":"<ul><li>What can we conclude when a patient has a nonischemic ECG and negative serial hs-troponins using an appropriate diagnostic pathway?<ul><li>\"The evaluation makes an acute MI unlikely or allows it to be ruled out. However, you may still have coronary artery disease that could explain your symptoms. We need to determine whether additional testing is appropriate.\"</li></ul></li><li>IP Evaluation of the Chest Pain Patient</li></ul>",
"69":"<ul><li>The HEART Pathway is a clinical decision pathway that combines the HEART score with a nonischemic EKG and serial negative troponins to identify patients who may be appropriate for early discharge.</li><li>HEART score<ul><li>History · EKG · Age · Risk factors · Troponin</li><li>A score of 0–3 indicates a lower-risk category.</li></ul></li><li>Confirm pathway criteria<ul><li>No ischemic EKG changes</li><li>Negative serial troponins</li><li>HEART score ≤3</li></ul></li><li>Low-risk definition: Estimated 30-day risk of death or MACE below 1%, using a validated clinical decision pathway</li><li>IP Evaluation of the Chest Pain Patient</li></ul>",
"70":"<ul><li>IP Evaluation of the Chest Pain Patient</li></ul><table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>HEART Score</td><td>HEART Pathway</td></tr><tr><td>Risk-stratification score (0–10)</td><td>Clinical decision pathway</td></tr><tr><td>Uses 5 components: History, ECG, Age, Risk factors, Troponin</td><td>Combines HEART/modified HEART score with EKG and serial troponins</td></tr><tr><td>Score of 0–3 = lower risk</td><td>Low risk generally requires score ≤3, no ischemic EKG changes, and negative serial troponins; the original pathway also excludes known CAD</td></tr></table>",
"71":"<ul><li>Chest Pain Rule-Out Admissions</li><li>LOW RISK: Discharge Home</li><li>Patient characteristics<ul><li>Nonischemic EKG</li><li>Negative hs-troponin pathway</li><li>Estimated 30-day risk of death or MACE below 1%</li></ul></li><li>Management<ul><li>No routine inpatient admission</li><li>No urgent stress testing or CCTA</li><li>Outpatient follow-up</li><li>Address cardiovascular risk factors</li></ul></li></ul>",
"72":"<ul><li>Chest Pain Rule-Out Admissions</li><li>INTERMEDIATE RISK: Additional Cardiac Evaluation</li><li>Patient characteristics<ul><li>Nonischemic or nondiagnostic EKG</li><li>Serial hs-troponins without evidence of acute MI</li><li>Persistent concern for CAD or ischemia</li></ul></li><li>Management<ul><li>Observation or admission for further evaluation</li><li>CCTA to assess coronary anatomy</li><li>Stress imaging to assess inducible ischemia</li><li>Select testing based on patient characteristics and clinical history</li></ul></li></ul>",
"73":"<ul><li>CCTA</li><li>Noninvasive imaging test that uses CT and IV contrast to visualize the coronary arteries.</li><li>Commonly used in patients with stable chest pain and an intermediate-to-high likelihood of CAD who have no known CAD.</li><li>Identifies coronary atherosclerotic plaque and evaluates the degree of coronary artery narrowing (stenosis).</li></ul>",
"74":"<ul><li>CCTA</li><li>Advantages<ul><li>Visualizes coronary artery anatomy and plaque.</li><li>Can identify both calcified and noncalcified plaque.</li><li>A normal study (no plaque or stenosis) makes obstructive CAD very unlikely.</li></ul></li><li>Limitations<ul><li>Requires IV iodinated contrast.</li><li>Image quality may be limited by extensive coronary calcification, irregular/rapid heart rate, or inability to adequately control heart rate.</li><li>Does not directly determine whether a coronary lesion causes myocardial ischemia.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"75":"<ul><li>Stress Test</li><li>When is stress testing used?</li><li>Used to evaluate for inducible myocardial ischemia in clinically stable patients with suspected or known CAD.</li><li>Increases myocardial oxygen demand or alters coronary blood flow to determine whether coronary perfusion is adequate.</li><li>Two approaches to stress testing:</li><li>Exercise stress testing</li><li>Pharmacologic stress testing</li><li>Stress testing may be performed with or without imaging:</li><li>Exercise ECG alone</li><li>Stress echocardiography</li><li>Nuclear myocardial perfusion imaging (MPI)</li><li>Stress cardiac MRI</li></ul>",
"76":"<ul><li>Stress Test</li><li>Stress testing may be used in the outpatient evaluation of clinically stable patients with suspected CAD and symptoms concerning for stable angina (not for active chest pain).</li><li>Jane is a 69-year-old woman who presents to her primary care provider after experiencing substernal pressure while walking uphill during a vacation in Hawaii two weeks ago. The discomfort resolved within several minutes of resting. She has had no recurrent symptoms and currently feels well.</li></ul>",
"77":"<ul><li>Pre-Test Probability Before Stress Testing</li><li>The estimated likelihood that a patient with chest pain has obstructive CAD before diagnostic testing.</li><li>Helps determine whether additional cardiac testing, including stress testing, is appropriate.</li><li>How is pretest probability estimated?<ul><li>Patient's age</li><li>Sex</li><li>Characteristics of presenting symptoms (e.g., exertional chest discomfort, relief with rest)</li><li>Additional clinical factors and clinician judgment</li></ul></li></ul>",
"78":"",
"79":"<ul><li>Pre-Test Probability Before Stress Testing</li><li>LOW probability (≤15%)</li><li>Additional testing may be deferred in selected patients.</li><li>Exercise EKG may be considered in selected patients.</li><li>INTERMEDIATE–HIGH probability (&gt;15%)</li><li>Noninvasive testing is more likely to be beneficial.</li><li>Options include stress testing or CCTA.</li></ul>",
"80":"<ul><li>Chest Pain Patient Care</li><li>A 65-year-old female presents with an episode of chest pain that was substernal and was provoked by exertion and was not relieved by rest or nitroglycerin.<ul><li>What is this patient’s pretest likelihood of having CAD?</li><li>Should she undergo cardiac stress testing, undergo left heart cath, or not undergo any cardiac testing and another diagnosis needs to be considered?</li></ul></li></ul>",
"81":"<ul><li>Stable Stan</li><li>Stan is a 46-year-old Caucasian male, typical of Ohio.  He played defensive line in college and bulked up at that time.  After graduating college with a degree in finance, he settled down at a desk job and has put on 50 lbs over time.</li><li>Stan is dragged to his wife’s PCP today for his first check-up in 25 years.  He admits that for at least 3 months now he has had left sided chest pain that limited his activity but was relieved by rest.  For example, he knows that if he mows the lawn without stopping for a beer after 20 minutes, he develops chest discomfort.</li></ul>",
"82":"<ul><li>Stable Stan</li><li>He is referred to a cardiologist and undergoes stress testing.</li><li>Stan’s stress test shows no EKG changes during Lexiscan infusion, however there is evidence of reversible ischemia reported on the MPI.</li><li>What does this mean?  What is the next step?</li></ul>",
"83":"<ul><li>Investigating a Complaint of Chest Pain</li><li>A stress test is used to risk stratify a patient with chest pain- and is a good rule out test.</li><li>This means that if a stress test is normal or negative, then the patient has a very low chance of having significant CAD.</li><li>However, when a stress test is positive- it means that we need another test- the left heart cath or coronary angiography.</li></ul>",
"84":"<ul><li>Investigating a Complaint of Chest Pain</li><li>Recall that a left heart catheterization is an invasive test, and that the test itself carries a small amount of risk.  Therefore, we use it only when the clinical circumstances are very compelling for CAD or when our initial non-invasive test cannot rule out CAD.</li><li>To have a cardiac cath, the patient must be able to lie flat fairly comfortably and must be NPO after midnight.</li></ul>",
"85":"<ul><li>Investigating a Complaint of Chest Pain</li><li>Once the left heart catheterization is underway, several outcomes are possible:<ul><li>No significant disease is found- this is a false positive stress test and does happen sometimes</li><li>The patient has disease that is amenable to stenting, and the LHC turns into both a diagnostic and therapeutic procedure.</li><li>The patient has disease that is better treated by medical or surgical management, and the catheterization remains diagnostic.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"86":"<ul><li>LHC Findings: Interpreting Coronary Anatomy</li><li>Angiography estimates how much the coronary artery lumen is narrowed.</li><li>≥70%</li><li>Non-left-main coronary artery stenosis is generally considered anatomically significant.</li><li>≥50%</li><li>Left main coronary artery stenosis is considered significant.</li><li>INTERMEDIATE STENOSES</li><li>For selected lesions, FFR ≤0.80 or iFR ≤0.89 indicates physiologic significance.</li></ul>",
"87":"<ul><li>LHC Findings: TIMI flow</li><li>TIMI flow is a grading system used during coronary angiography to describe how well blood flows through a coronary artery, particularly after an intervention such as PCI</li><li>Clinical takeaway</li><li>After successful PCI, the goal is restoration of TIMI 3 flow.</li></ul><table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>Grade</td><td>Description</td><td>Clinical meaning</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>No flow</td><td>Complete occlusion</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Penetration without perfusion</td><td>Minimal flow beyond obstruction</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Partial perfusion</td><td>Distal artery fills, but flow is delayed</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Normal perfusion</td><td>Normal coronary flow</td></tr></table>",
"88":"<ul><li>LHC Findings: Fractional Flow Reserve (FFR)</li><li>FFR measures the pressure beyond a coronary artery stenosis compared with the pressure before the stenosis.</li><li>This allows the cardiologist to determine whether stenosis is restricting blood flow enough to cause myocardial ischemia.</li></ul>",
"89":"<ul><li>LHC Findings: Guiding Treatment Decisions</li><li>The decision to revascularize depends on anatomy, ischemia, symptoms, and the patient.</li><li>MEDICAL THERAPY</li><li>• Guideline-directed risk reduction</li><li>CONSIDER PCI OR CABG</li><li>• Significant or high-risk anatomy</li></ul>",
"90":"<ul><li>Percutaneous Coronary Intervention (PCI)</li><li>How PCI restores coronary blood flow:</li><li>Balloon angioplasty: A catheter-mounted balloon is positioned across the stenosis and inflated to expand the narrowed segment.</li><li>Stent placement: A metal mesh stent is expanded to help maintain vessel patency.</li><li>Drug-eluting stent (DES): Most commonly used; releases medication that reduces neointimal growth and restenosis.</li><li>Bare-metal stent (BMS): A metal scaffold without medication that helps keep the artery open but has a higher risk of restenosis due to neointimal growth.</li></ul>",
"91":"<ul><li>Balloon Angioplasty</li><li>Percutaneous Coronary Intervention (PCI)</li></ul>",
"92":"<ul><li>Percutaneous Coronary Intervention (PCI)</li></ul>",
"93":"<ul><li>Percutaneous Coronary Intervention (PCI)</li><li>BSM vs DES</li><li>PANCE takeaway:</li><li>Contemporary DES are preferred over BMS for most patients because they reduce restenosis and repeat revascularization without increasing stent thrombosis risk.</li></ul><table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>Bare-metal stent (BMS)</td><td>Drug-eluting stent (DES)</td></tr><tr><td>Metal scaffold</td><td>Metal scaffold that releases medication</td></tr><tr><td>No antiproliferative drug</td><td>Medication limits neointimal growth</td></tr><tr><td>Higher restenosis risk</td><td>Lower restenosis risk</td></tr><tr><td>Older technology</td><td>Preferred in contemporary PCI</td></tr></table>",
"94":"<ul><li>Thrombosis vs Restenosis</li><li>In-stent thrombosis (Sudden event)</li><li>Acute clot formation within the stent.</li><li>Can occur hours to years after PCI.</li><li>Often presents as ACS or acute MI.</li><li>Risk factors include premature DAPT discontinuation, inadequate stent expansion, and malapposition.</li><li>In-stent restenosis (Usually gradual process)</li><li>Recurrent narrowing within the stented segment.</li><li>Primarily caused by neointimal hyperplasia; may also involve neoatherosclerosis.</li><li>May cause recurrent angina or ischemia.</li><li>Thrombosis = clot formation.</li><li>Restenosis = tissue growth and recurrent narrowing.</li></ul>",
"95":"<ul><li>Intracoronary Stents &amp; Dual Antiplatelet Therapy (DAPT)</li><li>Stent placement activates platelets: Balloon expansion and stent deployment injure the vessel lining and expose thrombogenic surfaces.</li><li>Risk of stent thrombosis: Platelets can aggregate on the stent before it becomes covered by healthy endothelial cells.</li><li>Preventing thrombosis: Dual antiplatelet therapy (DAPT) combines:<ul><li>Aspirin – inhibits platelet thromboxane A₂ production.<ul><li>P2Y12 inhibitor – blocks ADP-mediated platelet activation.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Common P2Y12 inhibitors:<ul><li>Thienopyridines: clopidogrel, prasugrel<ul><li>Non-thienopyridine: ticagrelor</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul>",
"96":"<ul><li>Duration of Dual Antiplatelet Therapy (DAPT) After PCI</li><li>ACS treated with PCI - 12 months</li><li>Aspirin + an oral P2Y12 inhibitor for at least 12 months in patients who are not at high bleeding risk.</li><li>Examples: STEMI, NSTEMI, and unstable angina treated with PCI.</li><li>Chronic coronary disease treated with PCI - 6 months</li><li>Aspirin + clopidogrel for 6 months, followed by single antiplatelet therapy</li></ul>",
"97":"<ul><li>CTO- Chronic Total Occlusions</li><li>A CTO is a 100% coronary artery occlusion with TIMI 0 flow that has been present for ≥3 months.</li><li>As coronary obstruction develops gradually, collateral vessels may enlarge to provide an alternative blood supply to the myocardium.</li><li>Collateral circulation may maintain blood flow at rest but may be insufficient to meet increased oxygen demand during exercise.</li><li>Some patients remain asymptomatic, while others develop exertional angina or myocardial ischemia.</li></ul>",
"98":"<ul><li>CTO- Chronic Total Occlusions</li><li>Treatment options include:</li><li>Optimal medical therapy: Antianginal medications and aggressive cardiovascular risk reduction.</li><li>CTO PCI: Specialized techniques allow interventional cardiologists to cross and reopen the occluded artery, followed by balloon angioplasty and usually DES placement.</li><li>CABG: Considered in selected patients based on coronary anatomy, symptoms, and overall disease burden.</li><li>When is CTO PCI considered?</li><li>Persistent, lifestyle-limiting angina despite optimal medical therapy.</li><li>Evidence of significant ischemia or viable myocardium in the territory supplied by the occluded artery.</li></ul>",
"99":"<ul><li>Complications of Cardiac Catheterization</li><li>Radial access (wrist)</li><li>Hematoma/bleeding</li><li>Radial artery occlusion</li><li>Arterial perforation</li><li>Rarely, compartment syndrome</li><li>Femoral access (groin)</li><li>Hematoma/bleeding</li><li>Pseudoaneurysm</li><li>AV fistula</li><li>Retroperitoneal hemorrhage</li></ul>",
"100":"<ul><li>Mechanism</li><li>Usually associated with a high femoral arterial puncture (above the inguinal ligament).</li><li>Blood accumulates in the retroperitoneal space, where it may not be externally visible.</li><li>Retroperitoneal hemorrhage</li><li>Potentially life-threatening complication</li></ul>",
"101":"<ul><li>Clinical presentation</li><li>Abdominal, flank, or back pain</li><li>Hypotension and tachycardia</li><li>Decreasing hemoglobin/hematocrit</li><li>May occur without visible groin swelling or bruising</li><li>Retroperitoneal hemorrhage</li><li>Retroperitoneal hemorrhage should be suspected if anemia or back pain develops after femoral vascular access.</li></ul>",
"102":"<ul><li>Diagnosis and management</li><li>CT/CT angiography of the abdomen and pelvis</li><li>Hemodynamic support and blood transfusion as indicated</li><li>Endovascular intervention or surgical repair if bleeding persists</li><li>Retroperitoneal hemorrhage</li></ul>",
"103":"<ul><li>Pseudoaneurysm</li><li>Pseudoaneurysm of the femoral artery - blood pools outside the femoral artery when if fails to close after the LHC but is held in place by the<ul><li>surrounding tissues.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"104":"<ul><li>Pseudoaneurysm</li><li>Pseudoaneurysm should be suspected when the patient develops pain out of proportion or swelling at the femoral access point.</li><li>Sometimes, a pulsatile mass or a bruit can be detected on exam.</li><li>Duplex ultrasound is the diagnostic test of choice for suspected femoral pseudoaneurysm</li></ul>",
"105":"<ul><li>Pseudoaneurysm</li><li>Treatment</li><li>Small, stable pseudoaneurysms (typically less than 2 cm): may resolve spontaneously with observation and follow-up ultrasound.</li><li>Ultrasound-guided thrombin injection: commonly preferred treatment for larger or symptomatic pseudoaneurysms.</li><li>Ultrasound-guided compression: alternative treatment.</li><li>Surgical repair: reserved for complicated cases or failure of less invasive treatment.</li></ul>",
"106":"<ul><li>AV Fistula</li><li>Abnormal connection between the femoral artery and vein.</li><li>Caused by inadvertent injury to both vessels during vascular access.</li><li>May present with a continuous bruit or palpable thrill.</li></ul>",
"107":"<ul><li>AV Fistula</li><li>Management</li><li>Observation: Many small, asymptomatic fistulas close spontaneously.</li><li>Ultrasound-guided compression: May be used for selected small fistulas.</li><li>Endovascular repair: Covered stent or embolization for selected cases.</li><li>Surgical repair: Reserved for complicated or persistent fistulas.</li></ul>",
"108":"<ul><li>Acute Kidney Injury</li><li>Contrast-associated acute kidney injury (CA-AKI):</li><li>Decline in kidney function occurring after exposure to iodinated contrast during coronary angiography.</li><li>May be related to contrast exposure or other factors, including hypotension, dehydration, and hemodynamic instability.</li><li>More likely to occur in patients with pre-existing CKD (stage IIIb or higher), diabetes.</li><li>Prevention is primarily with hydration and holding potentially nephrotoxic medications</li></ul>",
"109":"<ul><li>Cholesterol Emboli Syndrome</li><li>Occurs when cholesterol plaque is dislodged from the arterial wall and is swept downstream by arterial flow to small to medium arterioles.</li><li>LIVEDO RETICULARIS</li><li>Embolization of the cholesterol crystals not only creates mechanical plugging of the distal vessel, but also an intense inflammatory response.</li></ul>",
"110":"<ul><li>Cholesterol Emboli Syndrome</li><li>Livedo reticularis and blue toe syndrome (toe necrosis with intact distal pulses) are early cutaneous signs that may occur in cholesterol emboli syndrome.</li></ul>",
"111":"<ul><li>Cholesterol Emboli Syndrome</li><li>Patients with cholesterol emboli syndrome may also develop multiple other symptoms.</li><li>Constitutional symptoms of fever, anorexia, weight loss, fatigue, and myalgias are frequent manifestation of the inflammatory response.</li><li>Acute mesenteric ischemia- generalized abdominal pain out of proportion with no other findings on exam, lactic acidosis as a large amount of intestinal tissue is deprived of blood.</li></ul>",
"112":"<ul><li>Cholesterol Emboli Syndrome</li><li>Acute kidney injury from cholesterol emboli syndrome occurs later than the AKI seen in contrast induced nephropathy and is progressive.</li><li>Hypereosinophilia has been reported in up to 80% of the patients and occurs by an unknown mechanism.</li><li>Treatment of cholesterol emboli syndrome is primarily supportive.</li></ul>",
"113":"<ul><li>Coronary Artery Bypass Grafting</li><li>The saphenous vein, internal mammary artery, or radial artery are harvested and anastomosed to ascending aorta and coronary artery to BYPASS areas of atherosclerosis.</li><li>Sometimes, a patient with CAD is better served by a coronary artery bypass grafting procedure than by a stent.</li></ul>",
"114":"<ul><li>Coronary Artery Bypass Grafting</li><li>When is CABG preferred over PCI?</li><li>Significant left main disease: stenosis ≥50%.</li><li>Multivessel CAD with LV dysfunction:<ul><li>EF less than 35%: CABG recommended in appropriate patients.</li><li>EF 35–50%: CABG reasonable in selected patients.</li></ul></li><li>Diabetes with multivessel CAD involving the LAD: CABG preferred over PCI in appropriate surgical candidates.</li><li>Complex or diffuse multivessel disease: CABG may offer better long-term outcomes.</li></ul>",
"115":"<ul><li>Coronary Artery Bypass Grafting</li><li>Common bypass grafts</li></ul><table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>Conduit</td><td>Key point</td></tr><tr><td>LIMA (left internal mammary artery)</td><td>Preferred graft to LAD; approximately 90%+ 10-year patency</td></tr><tr><td>Radial artery</td><td>Preferred second arterial conduit in appropriate patients</td></tr><tr><td>Saphenous vein</td><td>Commonly used; approximately 50–60% 10-year patency</td></tr></table>",
"116":"<ul><li>Revascularization: CABG vs PCI</li><li>CABG BETTER:<ul><li>3 vessel CAD or left main disease</li><li>Multi-vessel disease in diabetics</li><li>Significant LV dysfunction and multi-vessel disease</li></ul></li><li>PCI BETTER:<ul><li>Limited number of lesions, normal ventricular systolic function</li></ul></li><li>Medical Therapy ONLY:<ul><li>Stable CAD without critical stenosis (&lt;70% for most arteries) and without LV systolic dysfunction</li></ul></li></ul>",
"117":"<ul><li>Further Management of CAD</li><li>Once the patient has undergone revascularization or if their disease is not severe enough to require revascularization, they will need additional medical management.</li><li>CAD patients are generally cared for by a cardiologist and typically see their cardiologist or cardiology APP every 3-6 months.</li><li>Management is focused on further risk factor modification and identifying any recurrent disease or complications.</li></ul>",
"118":"<ul><li>Medical Management of CAD/Angina Pectoris</li><li>Goal of therapy is:<ul><li>Slow/arrest the progression of coronary artery disease and thereby decrease the risk of ACS</li><li>Relieve anginal symptoms<ul><li>Lifestyle changes/Risk factor management</li><li>Revascularization (coronary angioplasty/stent vs CABG)</li><li>Pharmacotherapy</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>AGGRESSIVE risk factor reduction for CAD is the CORNERSTONE of therapy!!!</li></ul>",
"119":"<ul><li>Management Goals in CAD/Angina</li><li>Diabetes mellitus<ul><li>Goal A1c &lt; 7% (for some recommendation is &lt; 6.5%)</li></ul></li><li>Hyperlipidemia<ul><li>Goal LDL &lt; 100 (most recommend &lt; 70) STATINS</li><li>Goal Triglycerides &lt; 200 FIBRATES, FISH OIL</li></ul></li><li>Smoking cessation</li><li>Hypertension: control to &lt; 130/80</li><li>Obesity: weight &lt; 120% of IDBW (BMI &lt;= 24)</li><li>Sedentary Lifestyle: 30-60 mins moderate intensity exercise 5x/ week (walking, jogging, cycling).</li></ul>",
"120":"<ul><li>CAD Management: Mortality Lowering Pharmacologic Tx</li><li>Statin therapy: high intensity statin<ul><li>Atorvastatin (Lipitor) 40-80 mg daily</li><li>Rosuvastatin (Crestor) 20-40 mg daily</li></ul></li><li>Anti-platelet therapy<ul><li>Aspirin 81-325 mg daily, generally 81- 162mg preferred, use 81mg if GI effects<ul><li>PGY12 inhibitor daily in patients with ASA allergy</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul>",
"121":"<ul><li>Ongoing Care of the CAD Patient</li><li>Part of every cardiology visit will be to talk to the patient about chest pain they may be having and how much “rescue medication”, ie sublingual nitroglycerin they are using.</li><li>Ideally the patient is able to carry out normal activity without angina or using SL nitro.</li><li>Not all patients have angina monitor for anginal equivalents, like dyspnea and exercise symptoms.</li></ul>",
"122":"<ul><li>Canadian Cardiovascular Society (CCS) Classification of Angina</li></ul><table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>Class</td><td>Description</td></tr><tr><td>I</td><td>No limitation with ordinary activity. Angina occurs only with strenuous, rapid, or prolonged exertion.</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>Slight limitation of ordinary activity. Angina with brisk walking, walking uphill, climbing stairs rapidly, or activity after meals.</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>Marked limitation of ordinary activity. Angina with walking 1–2 blocks on level ground or climbing 1 flight of stairs at a normal pace.</td></tr><tr><td>IV</td><td>Inability to perform any physical activity without discomfort. Angina may occur at rest.</td></tr></table>",
"123":"<ul><li>Management of CAD: Symptom Management Pharmacologic Therapy</li><li>Anti-ischemic/Anti-Anginal medications:<ul><li>Beta-blockers (metoprolol, carvedilol)</li><li>Long-acting nitrates (isosorbide dinitrate)</li><li>Calcium channel blockers (diltiazem)</li><li>Ranolazine</li></ul></li><li>Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme Inhibitors/ARBs:<ul><li>Indicated for persons with history of DM or left ventricular systolic dysfunction</li></ul></li></ul>",
"124":"<ul><li>Anti-Ischemic/Anti-Anginal Medications</li></ul><table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>Drug Class</td><td>Example</td><td>Anti-Anginal Effect</td><td>Side Effects</td><td>Comment</td></tr><tr><td>Beta-Adrenergic blockers</td><td>Metorpolol, Atenolol, Bisoprolol</td><td>Decreases heart rate, contractility, blood pressure</td><td>Bradycardia Hypotension Bronchospasm Depression</td><td>May worsen heart failure and cause heart block, do NOT use in Variant Angina</td></tr><tr><td>Nitroglycerin</td><td>Sublingual Nitro, long acting nitrates</td><td>Decreases preload and afterload, coronary vasodilatation</td><td>Headache Flushing Orthostasis</td><td>Tachyphylaxis</td></tr><tr><td>Calcium Channel Blockers</td><td>Verapamil, Diltiazem</td><td>Decrease HR/BP Coronary Vasodilatation</td><td>Bradycardia Hypotension Constipation</td><td>May worsen heart failure, cause heart block</td></tr><tr><td>Late Sodium Current blocking  agents</td><td>Ranolazine</td><td>Prevents calcium overload</td><td>Dizziness Headache</td><td>No effect on BP/HR QTc prolongation</td></tr></table>"};
